  "response": "I worked from the scan JSON you supplied rather than re-running the CLI — flagging that so you know the verdicts below are only as fresh as that report.\n\n## Triage bar I applied\n\n| Category | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Block merge** | `severity: high`, or `agent_risk ≥ 0.5`, or evidence of an actual behavioural divergence between copies |\n| **Warn** | `severity: medium` **and** `agent_risk ≥ 0.30` **and** a plausible silent-drift story |\n| **Ignore** | `severity: low` / `agent_risk < 0.30`, or the finding is an artifact of this repo being a tutorial collection |\n\n**Nothing in this report meets the block bar.** `summary.high = 0`, and the ceiling on `scores.agent_risk` across all 83 findings is **0.41**. If your gate is \"block on crimes evidence,\" this PR passes. Everything below is warn-or-ignore.\n\n---\n\n## By detector\n\n### `magic_domain_literal_scatter` — 1 finding — **WARN**\n\n`crime_00001` · medium · `agent_risk 0.41` — the highest-risk finding in the report.\n\nThe literal `\"status\"` is loose across `actions.ts:43`, `create-form.tsx:97`, `edit-form.tsx:105` **inside `final-example` alone** (the other 2 files are the starter copy). That's genuine within-app scatter, not cross-example noise. It's the top-scoring finding because `severity 0.5 × confidence 0.85` survives without any blast-radius help. Warn, don't block: the fix is `fix_shape: \"reuse an existing definition\"`, and the suggested action itself carries `risk: medium` — a careless centralisation here is more dangerous than the scatter.\n\n### `duplicated_role_status_plan_check` — 2 findings — **WARN**\n\n`crime_00002` (`\"paid\"`) and `crime_00003` (`\"pending\"`) · medium · `agent_risk 0.36` each.\n\nThese are the closest thing to your \"repeated role checks,\" and worth reading carefully: **there are no role or permission checks in this scan at all.** These are invoice *status* comparisons. The real signal is the `2 distinct expression shapes` in the evidence — `invoice.status === \"paid\"` vs `status === \"paid\"` — across `edit-form.tsx` and `status.tsx` within one app. Two shapes for one rule is exactly how a status rule drifts when someone edits one call site. Warn.\n\nIf your reviewer brief was specifically authorization risk, this scan does not answer it — `coverage.detectors_default_off` includes `accessible_interaction_risk` and others, and there's no auth detector firing anywhere here.\n\n### `commented_out_code` — 10 findings — **WARN on 1, IGNORE on 9**\n\nFour are medium (`00004`–`00007`, `agent_risk 0.33–0.34`), six are low (`0.29–0.32`). Highest is `crime_00004`: 38 commented lines in `starter-example/app/ui/dashboard/latest-invoices.tsx`.\n\nNine of the ten sit in `dashboard/starter-example/` or are 2–5 line console/return stubs. In a Next.js *learn* starter, commented-out blocks are the exercise — the learner uncomments them. Deleting them destroys the repo's purpose. **Ignore** those.\n\nThe one I'd **warn** on is `crime_00007` — `starter-example/app/query/route.ts:21-25`, a commented `try/catch/await/return` in a route handler. A disabled error-handling path in a request handler is the shape most likely to get half-revived by the next agent that edits the file, which is precisely what `agent_risk` is measuring. Cheap to resolve (`effort: quick`, action `risk: low`): delete it or leave one line saying why.\n\n### `exact_duplicate_block` — 46 findings — **IGNORE (as a class)**\n\nThis is 55% of the report and I'd clear all of it. Every finding is medium / `confidence 0.95` / `agent_risk 0.28–0.31` — a flat band, which is the tell: they're all scoring identically because the detector is right about the hashes and the ranking has nothing to differentiate them with.\n\nRead the file pairs: `final-example` ↔ `starter-example`, `basics/api-routes-starter` ↔ `basics/basics-final` ↔ `basics/demo` ↔ `basics/dynamic-routes-step-1`, `seo` ↔ `seo/demo`. The duplication **is the product** — these are parallel tutorial checkpoints that must stand alone. `crime_00009` (Layout across 5 `basics/*` apps) and `crime_00010`–`00012` (`lib/posts.js` across 5) are the clearest cases. Acting on `extract_shared_helper` here would couple tutorial steps that are deliberately independent.\n\nThe top of this group (`00009`–`00012`, `agent_risk 0.31`) ranks slightly above the rest only because `blast_radius_direct_importers: 2`. That's a thin margin and it doesn't change the verdict.\n\nThese are worth recording as false positives — `crimes feedback <fingerprint> --verdict fp` — rather than re-triaging 46 items every review.\n\n### `duplicate_component_shape` — 1 finding — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00060` · medium · `agent_risk 0.28`, `confidence 0.70` (lowest confidence of any medium here). The `<div>` subtree spans `create-form` ↔ `edit-form` in both examples. Create/edit form pairs legitimately share shape while diverging in props; a structural-hash match at 0.70 confidence isn't enough to act on.\n\n### `direct_date` — 1 finding — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00061` · low · `agent_risk 0.28`. The evidence does the work: *\"none decide a branch — all 1 record or render the reading.\"* A single `new Date()` at `actions.ts:57` stamping an invoice date is the benign case the detector explicitly distinguishes. Note it if you ever add date-dependent tests; don't inject a clock for this.\n\n### `singular_plural_type_mismatch` — 2 findings — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00062/00063` · low · `agent_risk 0.28`, `confidence 0.70`. `revenue: Revenue[]` where \"revenue\" is a mass noun — it doesn't pluralise. The detector flags its own limits in evidence (*\"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"*). If it keeps surfacing, `detectors.options.singular_plural_type_mismatch.allowedNames`.\n\n### `deep_import` — 2 findings — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00064/00065` · low · `agent_risk 0.07`. `react-syntax-highlighter/dist/cjs/styles/prism` is that package's documented way to import a style — there is no public entry that exports it. `use_public_entry` is unactionable. Clear false positive; good `feedback --verdict fp` candidate.\n\n### `raster_should_be_vector` — 12 findings — **IGNORE**\n\nAll low, `agent_risk 0.05`, `test_gap 0`. Every file is `public/customers/<person-name>.png` at 56×56 or 60×60. These are **avatar photographs**, and the detector's premise — \"icon-sized, so it should be SVG\" — is simply wrong for photographic content. The size threshold fires; the inference behind it doesn't hold. False positive class.\n\n### `oversized_raster` — 6 findings — **WARN on 2, IGNORE on 4**\n\nAll low, `agent_risk 0.04` — the floor of the report. But `agent_risk` is the wrong lens here: these carry no agent-confusion risk at all, which is why they score last. Their cost is page weight, and `confidence` is 0.95 because it's a byte count, not an inference.\n\n**Warn** on `crime_00079` / `crime_00080` — `hero-desktop.png` at 486.6 KB in both examples, one notch under the medium threshold, and it's an above-the-fold hero. On a real dashboard PR that's a Core Web Vitals regression worth a comment. **Ignore** the 237 KB opengraph images (`00078`, `00081`) and the 422 KB `seo/**/large-image.jpg` — the latter is named `large-image` and is the SEO example's *subject matter*.\n\n---\n\n## Caveats on the evidence\n\nFour things constrain how much weight any of the above can bear:\n\n1. **`churn: 0` and `recency: 0` on every finding.** Against an empty baseline there's no history, so the recency component of ranking (`ranking.recency_enabled: true`) contributes nothing. These `agent_risk` values are essentially severity × confidence × blast radius.\n2. **`blast_radius: 0` on most findings is an artifact.** Next.js pages and route handlers are framework-loaded, not imported, so the import graph legitimately finds no importers. Read those zeros as \"not measurable here,\" not \"isolated.\"\n3. **`test_gap: 0.5` everywhere on code findings describes discovery, not coverage.** It means no associated tests were found, which is unsurprising in an examples repo. It is not evidence these paths are untested in any meaningful sense.\n4. **`detectors_default_off` includes `accessible_interaction_risk` and `design_token_escape`.** For a dashboard PR those are the two you'd most want on. This scan says nothing about a11y or design-token discipline — don't read a clean report as clearance on either.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nZero blocks, **5 warns** (`00001`, `00002`, `00003`, `00007`, and the `hero-desktop.png` pair), **78 ignores**. The two findings actually worth a reviewer's attention are the `\"status\"` scatter and the two-shapes-for-one-rule status checks — both inside `final-example`, both cheap, neither merge-blocking. The other 94% is a tutorial repo behaving exactly as a tutorial repo should, and I'd record the `exact_duplicate_block`, `deep_import`, and avatar-`raster_should_be_vector` groups as false positives so the next review starts from a much shorter list.",
